WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.000
Today, we're going to be speaking with James
Fletcher, who is the producer and director

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.720
of the documentary film The Accidental President.

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:08.879
I appear in this film.

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:15.709
I've played the trailer for my audience on
on the live stream that that I've done recently.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:19.300
And I appear very early in the trailer, which
I really appreciate.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.720
James, it's so it's so great to have you on.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.410
OK, so the accidental president, I think we
can sort of infer the idea that Donald Trump

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.919
wanted to run for president, not necessarily
to to be president.

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:32.480
Talk to us about the premise there.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.420
Well, I mean, it's it's like the plot of a
ridiculous Hollywood movie that got out of

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.420
control.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.879
I mean, I can attest this was an attention
seeking exercise that went crazy.

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:45.850
And Donald Trump was looking for a bit of
attention.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:52.120
And suddenly, by breaking all the rules, if
you like, of campaign law, he became the front

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.780
runner in the Republican field and the thing
ran away.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.510
And we all know what happened.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:02.579
So the title alludes to the fact that this
was nothing more than a bit of fun he was

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.660
having and suddenly it took on a whole life
of its own.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:13.190
In the film, you interview all sorts of folks
from sort of more pragmatic, traditional Republicans

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:19.110
all the way to what I guess I would call more
cultish true believers like Scott Adams of

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.990
Dilbert fame.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:26.250
Can you talk a little bit about what were
the trends you saw in terms of their perspective

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:32.150
on this presidency of Trump's comparing the
more traditional Republicans who have been

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:38.460
involved in the party for for some time versus
these kind of new entrants who who are really

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.960
Trump fans more than they are anything else?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.070
Well, of course, you know, the Trump won one
title replied with for the film was was the

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.070
hostile takeover.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.470
But I thought Trump would have liked that
too much.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.740
But he, in fact, did the same to the Republican
Party.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.580
And as you say, the Republican establishment
were shocked and completely unprepared for

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.580
his arrival.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:01.590
They didn't know how to deal with it or confront
him as a candidate.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.060
And, of course, they treated him for the most
part as a joke.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.209
So in finding our interviewees, we wanted
to look at people from all corners of the

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:12.209
establishment.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:16.180
We spoke to Matt Schlapp, the chairman of
CPAC, who I don't think was a Trump one's

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:18.299
really honest about it.

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:23.880
But then we went, as you say, to people like
Scott, Saddam's time wasn't really pro Trump

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.750
as he is now, because we wanted to illustrate
that inside the Republican Party, there were

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:35.200
two very different factions, you know, people
who believe in the GOP and people who believe

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.790
in Trump.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.299
Do you know who?

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:43.970
So there's all these stories of even up to
Election Day, Trump still didn't think he

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.990
was going to win and was shocked when when
he won.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.780
And and in contrast to that, there was the
Republican primary process in 2016, which

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.890
started with 17.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:59.889
And as it started to get whittled down, Trump
ended up sort of accruing the support from

00:02:59.889 --> 00:03:03.730
all of these people whose one, two, three
percent support ended up having to find a

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.109
new place to go.

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:10.180
Who figured out that Trump could win more
quickly the people immediately around Trump

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.329
or the Republican establishment that was watching
this?

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:14.349
That's a really good question.

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:16.030
I honestly don't know the answer.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:21.859
But what was explained to me was if you, for
example, hated or you loved Ted Cruz, but

00:03:21.859 --> 00:03:27.730
you hated, I don't know, Marco Rubio, you
thought, well, I'll put I'll put the votes

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.590
on Trump because he's going to he's not going
to do well anyway.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:31.860
He'll probably drop out.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.860
I said that.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:33.860
Sorry.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.950
That's a terrible example because they were
some of the last people standing.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:41.920
If you take if you take Rand Paul, for example,
there's one percent's right.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.999
You would transfer your one percent to Trump
because that was before Christmas and people

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:50.249
thought this is all a bit of fun, but it would
be it would be over fairly soon.

00:03:50.249 --> 00:03:55.139
And so Trump was blessed by this mathematical
advantage of having 16 opponents.

00:03:55.139 --> 00:04:00.579
And as these as these people fell out one
by one, almost for a bit of entertainment,

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:04.431
if I think a lot of people are really honest
with themselves, they put the money on Trump

00:04:04.431 --> 00:04:10.560
because he was such compelling viewing, so
entertaining in a field of Republicans, whether

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.849
the other 16 basically blurred into one and
I think had to take the entertainment value

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:18.600
as Trump is not to be underestimated in any
way.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:19.600
That's interesting.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.650
So some of that Trump support that shifted
earlier from the ones and twos, as we were

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:29.450
calling them at the time, were people who
were sort of thinking, eventually I will settle

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.830
on whoever is the nominee and it probably
will be somebody other than Trump.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:34.830
That's right.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.120
And this is a bit of entertainment along the
way.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.160
And we'll see where the cards land and then
we'll decide if we're going to be serious

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.160
about it.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.730
But, of course, you know, as is often the
case in politics, you need to put your stake

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:50.440
in the ground earlier, earlier in the process
rather than later.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.071
Do you did you get any kind of sense of when
and how the Republican establishment, when

00:04:56.071 --> 00:05:00.900
it became clear that it was going to be Trump
as the nominee, not as the eventual president,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:08.370
but as the nominee if how did they or did
they sort of race into action to try to get

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.200
control of the candidacy?

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:11.200
Well, that's right.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.030
And Matt Schlapp talks about this in the film.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.720
I mean, that panic happened again, too late.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.450
A lot of people treated Trump as a bit of
a sideshow.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:25.220
And so they didn't tackle him in the way he
would have been tackled, in my opinion, if

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.270
he were treated as a serious candidate from
day one.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.500
And a lot of people thought the damage he
could do to, for example, if I'm you know,

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.640
if I'm against Marco Rubio, it doesn't help
hurt me to think he's going to insult Marco

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.910
Rubio or Jeb Bush with the nicknames and run
them down.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.350
So he's actually doing me a favor.

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.640
And as long as he's in in the field, he's
probably good for me.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.910
But what a lot of people overlooked was the
fact that, as Molly Ball puts it, he would

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.340
run through the Republican field like a buzzsaw.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:58.780
And it was too late by the time people in
the Republican establishment realized this

00:05:58.780 --> 00:05:59.780
had happened.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.790
And it was, frankly, very little they could
do about it.

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:06.680
And certainly by the time he reached twelve
thirty seven, which people forget happened

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:07.750
before the convention.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:12.290
So he goes to the Republican convention as
the presumed nominee.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:17.120
Right, and one of the things that we've talked
about in terms of that process is that if

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.810
Donald Trump had run, as in the Democratic
primary, he never would have stood a shot.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:27.060
There are fundamental differences between
the Democratic primary electorate and the

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:31.970
Republican primary electorate, which even
if accounting for this idea of I wanted Rand

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.850
and he's out, I'll say Trump, but eventually
I'll go with whoever it is.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:39.580
Jeb, even if you account for that, Trump would
have never gone anywhere in the Democratic

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.580
primary.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:47.960
Did any of the people you spoke to in some
way comment on or understand that this was

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:52.830
only possible because of who Republican primary
voters are?

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:53.980
I think that's right.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.500
And, you know, it's also worth mentioning
that at the convention, there was an attempt

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.150
to destabilize the Trump candidacy.

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.880
And Paul Manafort got a lot of credit for
spotting those threats and seeing them off.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.750
But, yes, I mean, fundamentally, the difference
between the two parties was magnified and

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:18.600
made very clear, by the way, Trump was able
to march through that field effectively unchallenged

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.090
at this point.

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:26.240
Is there any sort of you can't argue with
success type of perspective because of what

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.230
Donald Trump was able to do?

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:32.970
Or does the fact that he was made only the
tenth one term president and this is this

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:38.350
is post filming of the documentary, but it's
sort of more of a now question.

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:42.980
Does the fact that he was made a one term
president in some way vindicate the people

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.510
who early on were not Trump people?

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:48.640
It's really interesting thought.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.550
But I mean, I would say one of the covid killed
Trump.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.230
I mean, I don't know where you stand on that,
but I think I agree.

00:07:56.230 --> 00:08:00.490
I think without covid, he probably would have
sailed through reelection.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.930
But nevertheless, for four years, his business
card, the president of the United States,

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:05.930
and he won.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:09.820
And you know, and as painful as that is for
a lot of people, you know, our film is looking

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:14.600
at how he won and how basically a game show
host became the most powerful person on the

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.590
planet because it happened.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:21.950
And admittedly, it was a perfect storm of
all kinds of luck, bizarre circumstances,

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:24.240
as you say, the Republican establishment.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.740
And that rules.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.680
But but he won and nearly won twice.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:34.490
And so I can attest there is a very solid
basis on which to launch an inquiry in this

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:37.260
case, a documentary called The Accidental
President.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:42.669
What do you think is the desire of the more
traditional Republican Party insiders?

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:46.490
That would be the future of the Republican
Party, because now there's this question with

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:50.680
people like Madison, Kathyrn and Lauren Bobert
and Marjorie Taylor Green, who really are

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:56.310
only in Congress because they were on Trump's
side and they were simpatico to Trump.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.949
We don't know whether they will be reelected
or not in twenty, twenty two.

00:08:58.949 --> 00:09:03.290
And there's a real question as to whether
the Republican Party will remain a Trump party

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:08.189
or go back to what it was before the more
traditional insider types that you interviewed

00:09:08.189 --> 00:09:09.189
in the film.

00:09:09.189 --> 00:09:10.189
What do you think they want now?

00:09:10.189 --> 00:09:15.110
Well, I think it's I think 20 22 will be really
interesting if if the Republicans come storming

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:20.710
back and Trump in some way as this de facto
leader of the opposition, I know that's not

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:21.710
enough.

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:22.889
It's obviously in American politics.

00:09:22.889 --> 00:09:28.090
But he's put himself there if they do very
well in twenty twenty two and his people and

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:34.050
he can take the credit for it with the get
whatever credibility that I think Trump stays.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:37.910
And it'll be very difficult to dismiss him.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:42.930
However, if it's a wipeout in twenty, twenty
two, I think people in the Republican establishment

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:46.379
would say Trump is now a massive liability
and we've got to find a way of getting rid

00:09:46.379 --> 00:09:48.209
of him presence.

00:09:48.209 --> 00:09:52.920
I wonder if that you know, at the CPAC speech,
there was the rumor Trump might announce for

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.449
2024 and then, of course, he didn't.

00:09:55.449 --> 00:09:59.001
But he did announce, you know, you can donate
money on his website and he's going to be

00:09:59.001 --> 00:10:01.020
supporting candidates in 2022.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:05.620
It almost seems like a trial balloon where
if he can continue to sort of play kingmaker

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:10.939
and wield influence successfully in 2022,
maybe it makes his 2004 run more likely.

00:10:10.939 --> 00:10:15.790
And if it doesn't work, then he can not necessarily
gracefully exit, but at least exit.

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:16.790
That's right.

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:20.319
And of course, David, you know, if he were
to run in 2020 for one, for, as he put it

00:10:20.319 --> 00:10:26.720
a third time, I'm sure you'd have no contest
at that point.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.269
Yeah, but yeah, I think I do think 2022 will
be the moment at which the Republican Party

00:10:32.269 --> 00:10:36.769
will either decide we've got to hang onto
him even if we can't stand the thought, because

00:10:36.769 --> 00:10:38.600
he does win for us.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:42.689
And this is all you know, this is not my opinion.

00:10:42.689 --> 00:10:49.140
But if if it's a wipeout, then I think I mean,
the problem is there isn't an obvious candidate,

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:53.259
which you would think by now that might be
so 2022.

00:10:53.259 --> 00:10:56.550
I think he's going to be a pivotal moment
for the Republican establishment.

00:10:56.550 --> 00:11:01.999
How much does all of this, number one, depend
on and will be guided by what's going on in

00:11:01.999 --> 00:11:03.310
other parts of the world?

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:07.199
Because at the time of Trump, we also had
the rise of some of these nationalist right

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:12.339
wing parties in different parts of Europe,
the Boris Johnson ization.

00:11:12.339 --> 00:11:16.960
In England and in all of these different things,
and that that also may change over the next

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:22.149
two to four years, does it is Trump big enough
now that it doesn't matter anymore?

00:11:22.149 --> 00:11:25.529
Or is there still some kind of global picture
here that will dictate?

00:11:25.529 --> 00:11:29.870
I don't know if I can correct me if you disagree,
but I'm not sure that what's happening outside

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.860
of America particularly informs what happens
inside America.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:39.819
I mean, the Brexit vote for me was an indication
that this that Trump could happen.

00:11:39.819 --> 00:11:40.819
Right.

00:11:40.819 --> 00:11:44.791
But I don't think it's possible to say that
Trump happened because of Brexit or that it

00:11:44.791 --> 00:11:46.189
paved the way.

00:11:46.189 --> 00:11:51.580
I think the conditions in America were amenable
to Trump win, which wasn't as obvious at the

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.559
time as it was in retrospect.

00:11:54.559 --> 00:11:57.939
So I'm not sure global politics will have
much bearing.

00:11:57.939 --> 00:12:03.430
Yeah, I'm not inclined to at least not to
disagree strongly in terms of the production

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:04.430
of the film.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:07.920
And we're talking with James Fletcher about
the documentary The Accidental President.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:11.480
You produced it over quite a period of time.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:14.649
What is the how do you even manage logistically?

00:12:14.649 --> 00:12:16.500
You have all of these interviews.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.949
You have the big picture story you want to
tell.

00:12:19.949 --> 00:12:24.600
I'm trying to figure out what how how are
you organized the day to day to end up putting

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:29.079
the story you want to tell together from all
the footage that you have?

00:12:29.079 --> 00:12:33.709
What happened was we finished our last interview
with Kellyanne Conway the very day Trump announced

00:12:33.709 --> 00:12:35.540
locked down in the United States.

00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:40.069
So that was, I think, early March of last
year.

00:12:40.069 --> 00:12:41.069
Yeah.

00:12:41.069 --> 00:12:44.100
And we started editing literally a week before
lockdown.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.810
I just appointed the editor.

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:51.749
And so I never spent any time in the same
room as him, which is extraordinary and unheard

00:12:51.749 --> 00:12:53.809
of when working on any film.

00:12:53.809 --> 00:12:55.579
And the same is true of everyone else in post-production.

00:12:55.579 --> 00:12:57.680
The composer was in Los Angeles.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:59.269
The sound design was done in London.

00:12:59.269 --> 00:13:00.990
The color was of the pitch.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:05.240
It was done in Texas by a guy from New York
who was in lockdown with his wife's family.

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:07.869
So it was a real challenge.

00:13:07.869 --> 00:13:14.220
And all the meetings that you would normally
have in person and trial and error and creative

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.139
ideas weren't possible in the normal way.

00:13:17.139 --> 00:13:18.829
But we got there.

00:13:18.829 --> 00:13:24.509
And but we I think what you're asking is we
shot the film from June 15 until Election

00:13:24.509 --> 00:13:25.509
Day of 2016.

00:13:25.509 --> 00:13:27.660
So in a way, it didn't really matter.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:32.660
The only thing I was personally disappointed
was that we had to release the film later

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:33.899
than I would like to.

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:36.059
But it's a balanced piece anyway.

00:13:36.059 --> 00:13:39.770
I don't think it would have made any difference
to the outcome of the election had we released

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:45.059
that, for example, in September, as people
in CA just encouraged us to.

00:13:45.059 --> 00:13:49.259
I think if I wanted to affect the election,
I'd have had to basically to hit job on Trump.

00:13:49.259 --> 00:13:51.149
And that's not what this film is.

00:13:51.149 --> 00:13:54.040
When you're sitting there and you're putting
it together.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:58.869
And as people will see when they watch that,
there will be I'll say something and then

00:13:58.869 --> 00:14:02.070
it'll cut to somebody else and then a third
person.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:07.339
Are you working off of transcripts to say,
we want this line and then this line?

00:14:07.339 --> 00:14:10.959
Are you going from memory from the interviews,
which seems like it would be so difficult

00:14:10.959 --> 00:14:11.959
to do?

00:14:11.959 --> 00:14:13.360
It was a bit of both.

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:19.240
I mean, I, I did all the transcripts myself,
so in my head I had all the lines I liked.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.279
And then I went through the transcripts with
literally pen and paper.

00:14:22.279 --> 00:14:27.279
And I put lines on the for example, all the
things I wanted to put in the film that you

00:14:27.279 --> 00:14:32.259
said that of course, you were into the fact
that it's just under a hundred and twenty

00:14:32.259 --> 00:14:33.820
minutes, one hundred and ten minute film.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:38.720
And so sadly, when you've got twenty, almost
one hour interviews, a lot of things don't

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:40.939
make it into the final cut, which is really
frustrating.

00:14:40.939 --> 00:14:47.819
And then, you know, people have asked this
is should this have been a 10 part limited

00:14:47.819 --> 00:14:48.819
series?

00:14:48.819 --> 00:14:51.170
I'm not sure people that have the stomach
for that show.

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:56.029
I would have had to stomach to make it and
watch as much trouble as I did.

00:14:56.029 --> 00:14:58.610
But we didn't have a plan.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:01.379
And everyone approaches documentary making
a different way.

00:15:01.379 --> 00:15:08.920
But I think between me and Noah, the editor,
it was really the two of us who did this whole

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:09.920
thing.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.069
I don't see massive credit to him.

00:15:12.069 --> 00:15:19.449
Yeah, we we just pull it on knows we we probably
would not get a film school for the way we

00:15:19.449 --> 00:15:24.059
did it, but I just felt it had to feel very
natural and keep moving.

00:15:24.059 --> 00:15:29.709
And so we were brutal in that if someone was
waffling or going on too long and the sentence

00:15:29.709 --> 00:15:30.759
was cut in half.

00:15:30.759 --> 00:15:31.759
Right.

00:15:31.759 --> 00:15:33.550
That's how most of my footage ended up being
cut.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.420
In fact, because of the waffling.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:40.170
No, possible no, no.

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:41.829
It's a it's an excellent documentary.

00:15:41.829 --> 00:15:46.380
We're talking with James Fletcher, the producer
and director of The Accidental President.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:50.699
And where are the easiest places for people
to find the film?

00:15:50.699 --> 00:15:53.629
So it's on Apple TV in 72 countries.

00:15:53.629 --> 00:15:57.879
We've translated the subtitles into Latin
American Spanish.

00:15:57.879 --> 00:16:01.199
It's also on Amazon and Google Play.

00:16:01.199 --> 00:16:03.750
The whole Android platform is covered as well.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:04.930
And YouTube.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:06.889
James, thank you so much for talking to us
about it.

00:16:06.889 --> 00:16:06.899
Thank you very much,

